// Package tileshape derives collision and lighting geometry from a tilemap: a View that physics.MoveAndSlide, physics.CharacterBody, the platformer controller and physics.World take as their physics.TileGrid, the physics.Slope segments of slanted and partial tiles, the merged outline of solid cells and tile shapes, static boxes, and lights2d occluders.
//
// A cell collides as a whole when its tiles carry TileSolid or TileOneWay, from SetFlags, a "solid" or "oneWay" tile property, or a SetCellFlags override, or when one of its tiles has a collision shape that fills the cell. A tile whose collision shapes (tilemap.Tileset.Shapes, such as the ones Tiled's collision editor draws) do not fill the cell collides through those shapes instead: the cell reports neither TileSolid nor TileOneWay to the grid, and the shapes' upward-facing and vertical edges become slopes, so a character walks 45 degree, 2:1 and half-height tiles with no hand-placed segments. A SetCellFlags override takes precedence over every tile in the cell, and shapes follow the flip bits of the tile that placed them.
//
// Geometry is cached in chunks of 16 by 16 cells and built on first use. A View follows the map's change log (Tilemap.TileRevision and Tilemap.TileChangesSince), so after SetTile, FillRect, SetCellFlags or ClearCellFlags its next call rebuilds only the chunks around the cells that changed, and after SetFlags every cell. After writing a layer's Data directly call View.Invalidate for the cells written, and after editing a tileset's Shapes call View.InvalidateAll.
//
// Only bounded orthogonal maps are supported: a View of an unbounded map (Tilemap.Infinite, or a sparse chunked layer) has no cells, and layer offsets are ignored, as they are by the tilemap's own collision queries. A polyline shape is an open edge a character stands on, never part of the outline or the occluders, and never makes its cell solid.
package tileshape
